  "textEditorsPageArticleIntro": "<b>A report card</b> is a document that tells a student and their family how the student is doing at <school>school</school>. It lists each subject with a <grade>mark</grade>, a short remark written by the teacher, and a total at the foot of the page.",
  "@textEditorsPageArticleIntro": {},
  "textEditorsPageArticleLayout": "The layout has stayed close to the printed form: ruled rows, small capital labels and a signature line, so a card from today still reads like one from <i>fifty years ago</i>.",
  "@textEditorsPageArticleLayout": {},
  "textEditorsPageArticleQuote": "“A mark says where a student stands. The remark says what to do next, and that is the line a family reads twice.”",
  "@textEditorsPageArticleQuote": {},
  "textEditorsPageArticleQuoteIntro": "A form tutor at a secondary school described the remark like this:",
  "@textEditorsPageArticleQuoteIntro": {},
  "textEditorsPageArticleTerms": "Report cards go home at the end of each term. Many schools now publish them online as well as on paper, so families can read them on the day they are signed.",
  "@textEditorsPageArticleTerms": {},
